1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,432
This is Ajaia Docs.

2
00:00:01,432 --> 00:00:06,784
A document editor with autosave,
file import, and per document sharing.

3
00:00:06,784 --> 00:00:11,458
The dashboard splits what you
own from what's shared with you.

4
00:00:11,458 --> 00:00:14,248
Then the decision I'd defend hardest.

5
00:00:14,648 --> 00:00:15,840
Editing is TipTap.

6
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:20,278
Bold, italic, underline, three
heading levels, and both list types.

7
00:00:20,278 --> 00:00:25,776
Every change is debounced and saved
automatically, seven hundred fifty milliseconds

8
00:00:25,776 --> 00:00:31,009
after you stop typing. That's the
indicator up here going from Saving to Saved.

9
00:00:31,009 --> 00:00:35,515
This isn't a text area. Underneath
it's a structured document model.

10
00:00:35,915 --> 00:00:38,921
Hard refresh. It's all still there.

11
00:00:38,921 --> 00:00:45,620
Content lives in Postgres as the
editor's structured J S O N, never as markup.

12
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:48,455
That choice pays off twice later.

13
00:00:48,855 --> 00:00:53,937
Import takes plain text, capped at
two hundred fifty six kilobytes.

14
00:00:53,937 --> 00:01:00,157
Look at the last line. That's a script
tag, and it renders as text, not as markup.

15
00:01:00,157 --> 00:01:04,025
The importer puts every
character into a text node.

16
00:01:04,025 --> 00:01:08,500
There's no path where an
uploaded file becomes live markup.

17
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:14,736
Sharing is by email. Ada owns this
document. She's giving Grace access. Two roles.

18
00:01:14,736 --> 00:01:18,223
Owner reads, edits,
renames, deletes, and shares.

19
00:01:18,223 --> 00:01:20,928
Collaborator reads and edits the body.

20
00:01:20,928 --> 00:01:26,764
Renaming is owner only, because the title
is how this document shows up on someone

21
00:01:26,764 --> 00:01:27,974
else's dashboard.

22
00:01:28,374 --> 00:01:33,112
Grace opens the same document.
Her avatar appears in Ada's header.

23
00:01:33,112 --> 00:01:35,768
This is a heartbeat, not a websocket.

24
00:01:35,768 --> 00:01:39,142
Each client writes a row
every fifteen seconds.

25
00:01:39,142 --> 00:01:44,813
Cloud Run scales to zero, so a container
has nowhere to hold a live connection.

26
00:01:44,813 --> 00:01:49,981
A socket would mean a second always
on service for something decorative.

27
00:01:49,981 --> 00:01:52,206
Someone can appear a poll late.

28
00:01:52,206 --> 00:01:56,010
The platform makes the
cruder design the correct one.

29
00:01:56,410 --> 00:02:01,107
Grace types and saves. Ada types
next, on a copy that's already stale.

30
00:02:01,107 --> 00:02:05,468
Her save is refused with a four
oh nine, and this banner appears.

31
00:02:05,468 --> 00:02:10,769
Every save carries the timestamp it was
based on, so a stale write matches zero

32
00:02:10,769 --> 00:02:15,332
rows. Silent clobbering was the
worst thing about the first version.

33
00:02:15,732 --> 00:02:19,544
Grace's window. No Share
button, no Delete button.

34
00:02:19,544 --> 00:02:24,118
Alan, who this was never
shared with, opens the document id.

35
00:02:24,118 --> 00:02:27,473
He gets a four oh
four, not a four oh three.

36
00:02:27,473 --> 00:02:31,285
A four oh three would
confirm the document exists.

37
00:02:31,285 --> 00:02:37,308
Every access goes through one data
layer, and the permission check lives in the

38
00:02:37,308 --> 00:02:38,299
where clause.

39
00:02:38,699 --> 00:02:42,604
History lists every version
with an author and a time.

40
00:02:42,604 --> 00:02:47,955
Snapshots happen per editing session,
not per save, because autosave fires

41
00:02:47,955 --> 00:02:53,740
constantly. Restoring takes a snapshot
first, so the restore is itself undoable.

42
00:02:53,740 --> 00:02:57,861
A restore you can't undo is
worse than no history at all.

43
00:02:58,261 --> 00:03:04,866
Export walks the J S O N into Markdown,
escaping anything Markdown would read as

44
00:03:04,866 --> 00:03:08,911
formatting. The mirror of
the importer's promise.

45
00:03:09,311 --> 00:03:14,132
Real time co editing was cut on
purpose. A half working C R D T loses data.

46
00:03:14,132 --> 00:03:16,253
That's a boundary, not a backlog.

